  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 AM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nExpect a mix of wet and dry storms today with strong gusty \noutflow winds possible near storms today. Most of the activity \nshould diminish tonight.\n\nOn Thursday, a low pressure system will eject out of California and \nadvance towards south Idaho. Models are showing a band of showers \nwith isolated to scattered thunderstorms within and around this band \nof showers. This is expected to arrive Thursday afternoon with \nmodels showing quite a bit of moisture across the eastern highlands \nas this feature moves north. Potentially this could bring 0.25 to \n0.50 inches of rain just Thursday afternoon. Confidence is moderate \nto high of appreciable rain on the Big Rock fire east of Idaho \nFalls. So have decided to issue a flood watch for the burn. While \nother burn scars may need watches as well with this feature moving \nnorth, this particular burn scar is the most likely to see heavy \nrain assuming the track of the low doesn't shift appreciably. \n\nAs we move into Friday, a secondary weaker trough will arrive from \nthe southwest and provide the trigger for additional convection. The \nmodels seem to be favoring the central mountains into the upper \nSnake River highlands for appreciable rainfall with over 0.10 inches \nforecast Friday afternoon. So we may need additional flood watches \nfor burn scars and possibly for the Big Rock again. On Saturday, one \nadditional shortwave trough will move through the region resulting \nin scattered showers and thunderstorms with the main focus once \nagain across the central mountains into the Island Park region. With \nthe monsoon air firmly in place, the threat for flash flooding on \nthese burn scars will likely continue. As we head into Sunday and \nMonday, a ridge retrogrades from the east and and builds across the \narea. This will shut down the \"organized\" thunderstorms. But the \nmonsoon moisture will remain in place. So we'll probably keep \ncontinued chances for isolated storms into early next week. The \nforecast at this point could very well be too dry on pops with the \nmodels assuming too strong of a cap in place.\n\nFrom a temperature perspective, today is the last warm day with \ncloud cover and showers providing cooler temperatures into the \nweekend. As we head into next week, temperatures rebound into the \n80s but not the extreme heat that we've been experiencing.",
